In this lesson, we'll look at how to pick the right measure of spread for your data. The big idea is simple — match the measure to the shape of the distribution. If your data is roughly symmetric with no extreme values, the standard deviation is really informative, because it uses every single data point. But if the data is skewed or has outliers, the IQR, that's the interquartile range, is much more reliable, and the standard deviation can actually mislead you. Why? Because the standard deviation squares the deviations from the mean, so one value sitting way out on its own can completely dominate the result, making the spread look larger than it really is. When you just want to compare two groups side by side, boxplots are your best friend — they show the median and IQR at a glance. And if you're explaining results to a general audience, the range is easy for everyone to grasp. Just remember, when comparing two data sets, always use the same measure for both, otherwise the comparison is meaningless. Finally, with a small sample, your spread estimate might bounce around a lot, but it stabilises as your sample grows. Now let's see it in action.